{
  "passages": [
    {
      "reference": "1",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": false,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": true
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "ἐπὶ τοῦ ἐνεστῶτος πονηροῦ",
          "english_quote": "before the evil one now in power",
          "issue": "With the personal-ruler interpretation adopted by the draft, ἐπί with the genitive naturally locates the proclamation under or during that ruler's ascendancy. 'Before' instead introduces a presence or confrontation relationship not established by the Greek. The note discloses the personal versus impersonal interpretation, but does not justify this prepositional rendering.",
          "suggested_revision": "Consider 'under the present evil one' or 'during the rule of the present evil one.' Retain the disclosed alternative referring more generally to present evil. This is a lexical issue, not evidence of corruption in the supplied print.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "αὐτὸς τοῦ νῦν βίου βιαίως τὸ ζῆν μετήλλαξεν",
          "english_quote": "he himself, through violence, exchanged life in the present existence.",
          "issue": "The Greek uses exchange-language as an expression for leaving life, here explicitly through violence. The English leaves an unexplained exchange and does not intelligibly communicate the death asserted by the source. The lexical note recognizes death, but the main text does not convey it.",
          "suggested_revision": "Render the death clearly, for example, 'he himself left this present life through violence,' and preserve the literal exchange-language of μετήλλαξεν in a note. Do not supply an unspecified destination or a claim about the life received in exchange. The replacement requires fresh review.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "2",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "ἵνα περὶ παντὸς οὗ ἂν χειροτονήσῃ ἐπὶ γῆς ἔσται δεδογματισμένον ἐν οὐρανοῖς",
          "english_quote": "so that whatever he decrees on earth will stand decreed in the heavens.",
          "issue": "Using 'decrees' and 'decreed' conceals the distinction between χειροτονήσῃ, involving selection, appointment, or determination, and δεδογματισμένον, expressing an established decree. It also hides the recurrence of χειροτονέω after 'I ordain this Clement.' Context supports decisions about matters, but παντὸς οὗ can grammatically be personal as well; the note's categorical exclusion of appointing a person is insufficiently qualified.",
          "suggested_revision": "Compare a rendering such as 'so that whatever he determines on earth will stand decreed in the heavens.' Explain the contextual choice of 'determines' alongside the earlier 'ordain,' and preserve the possible appointment-related reading, 'concerning anyone whom he appoints,' rather than ruling it out without further argument. Retain the distinction between the earthly act and the heavenly decree. Review the revised wording afresh.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "3",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "ἡ κρείττων ἀπαρχή",
          "english_quote": "the choicest firstfruits",
          "issue": "'Choicest' is an English superlative, whereas κρείττων continues the passage's repeated comparative language about someone 'better.' Context may support a superlative interpretation, but the note neither argues that interpretation nor correctly describes 'choicest' as retaining comparative force.",
          "suggested_revision": "Compare 'the superior firstfruits' or 'the more excellent firstfruits.' If a superlative rendering is retained, document why the context warrants it and preserve the comparative alternative. Do not describe 'choicest' itself as comparative.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "σὺ γὰρ δι᾽ ἐμοῦ τῶν σωζομένων ἐθνῶν ἡ κρείττων ἀπαρχή",
          "english_quote": "for through me you are the choicest firstfruits of the nations being saved.",
          "issue": "The English places 'through me' with Clement's being firstfruits. The Greek also naturally permits, and context favors, connecting Peter's agency with the nations' being saved. That difference affects which saved people form the comparison group. Neither the main text nor the notes disclose this attachment question.",
          "suggested_revision": "Consider 'for you are the superior firstfruits of the nations being saved through me,' while noting the alternative attachment, 'through me you are ...'. Record this as interpretive uncertainty in source_issues, separately from the already disclosed editorial punctuation in 'ὁ δ᾽. ἀπεκρίνατο'. No textual correction is established, and the revised sentence needs fresh review.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "4",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": false,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": true
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "οἶδα δὲ καὶ αὐτός, ὦ Κλήμης, λύπας καὶ ἀθυμίας καὶ κινδύνους καὶ ψόγους τοὺς ἐξ ἀπαιδεύτων ὄχλων σοι δωρούμενος",
          "english_quote": "I myself know, Clement, that in giving you griefs, discouragements, dangers, and reproaches from uneducated crowds, I am presenting you with gifts.",
          "issue": "The source expresses Peter's awareness that he is bestowing these burdens on Clement. The draft expands the single bestowing expression into 'in giving ... I am presenting ... with gifts,' making the object of his knowledge an explicit appraisal of the burdens as gifts. The gift-language is present in δωρούμενος, but this additional explanatory proposition shifts the sentence's emphasis and claim.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use one gift-related verb while keeping the burdens as its objects: 'I myself know, Clement, that I am bestowing on you griefs, discouragements, dangers, and reproaches from uneducated crowds.' A note can explain the pointed gift-language without turning it into an additional assertion in the main text. Review the replacement afresh.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "5",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "οὐ γὰρ κριτὴν καὶ δικαστὴν χρημάτων ἢ ἀσχολημάτων καθεστάναι σε θέλει τῶν νῦν βιωτικῶν πραγμάτων ὁ χριστός, ἵνα συνεχόμενος εἰς τὰς νῦν τῶν ἀνθρώπων φροντίδας μὴ εὐσχολῇς χωρίζειν λόγῳ ἀληθείας ἀνθρώπων τοὺς κρείττονας ἀπὸ τῶν χειρόνων",
          "english_quote": "For the Messiah does not want you appointed as a judge and arbiter of financial or business matters among the worldly affairs of the present, so that, absorbed in people’s present concerns, you have no leisure to separate, by the word of truth, the better people from the worse.",
          "issue": "The placement of 'so that' leaves the negative purpose or consequence insufficiently scoped under 'does not want.' English readers can understand the lack of leisure as the purpose of refusing the appointment, rather than as part of the rejected scenario of appointment and preoccupation. The unusual οὐ ... θέλει ... ἵνα ... μὴ construction needs an explicit interpretive account, not an unqualified reproduction of the ambiguity in English.",
          "suggested_revision": "Consider 'For the Messiah does not want you appointed as a judge and arbiter of financial or business matters among the worldly affairs of the present and thereby, absorbed in people’s present concerns, left without leisure to separate, by the word of truth, the better people from the worse.' Explain in source_issues that this takes the ἵνα clause as the unwanted outcome within the negated desire, preserving the supplied μὴ. This is a scope/meaning question, not established source corruption. Freshly review the revised sentence.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
